/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE GMP INTERIOR of the production building.  The plot plan above sees the
  building as ONE box, the `productionBuilding` item; `envelope
  productionBuilding;` makes that box the SHELL of the rooms below, so every
  room must lie inside it (x -70..-26 m, y -18..18 m, z 0..10 m), and the
  items the rooms list stand inside it.  Grammar and checks:
  docs/ai/case-layout.md, "THE GMP INTERIOR"; record
  docs/design/a-gmp-interior-is-declared-and-checked.md.

  WHAT GRADE, AND WHY.  This is an ORAL SOLID DOSE (film-coated tablet)
  suite, not a sterile one.  EU GMP Annex 1, whose grade vocabulary A/B/C/D
  the engine speaks, is written for STERILE products; nothing in it applies
  to tablets by its own terms.  The rooms where product is exposed
  (dispensing, granulation, drying, milling, blending, compression, coating
  and its preparation, primary packaging, the corridors and airlocks that
  serve them, the IPC and wash rooms) are declared grade D BY ANALOGY, the
  usual practice for an OSD plant, and every other room CNC (controlled,
  not classified).  That is this case's statement, to be replaced by the
  company's own risk assessment -- not a value looked up.

  THE CASCADE.  Every pressure is declared to outside, as in
  apalutamide01_tablets: CNC rooms +15 Pa, the clean corridor +35 Pa,
  gowning and the cascade airlock between their neighbours, the personnel
  bubbles +45 Pa, the material sinks +5/+10 Pa, the contained suite's own
  corridor +25 Pa and its process rooms +15 Pa, so air moves INTO the rooms
  where powder is open.  The step is Annex 1's guidance value, borrowed.

  CONTAINMENT.  The API is BOUGHT and its occupational exposure band is NOT
  KNOWN to this case.  Every room where the API is handled as an OPEN
  powder -- dispensing, granulation, drying, milling, final blending,
  compression -- is declared contained, as a PRECAUTION until the
  supplier's safety data sheet gives the band: CONFIRM THE BAND (README
  section 9).  A drug dosed at 10 mg a day is pharmacologically active at
  milligram doses, and that is the whole argument for the precaution; if
  the band turns out low, the bubbles and sinks may become cascades and the
  suite shrinks.  Coating and packaging handle tablets, not open powder,
  and are not contained.

  NO HAZARDOUS AREA.  There is no flammable solvent anywhere in the plant
  (an aqueous binder, an aqueous coat), so no room carries an `atex {}`
  zone.  The fine organic powders (lactose, starch, the API) are
  COMBUSTIBLE DUSTS: whether the fluid bed, the mill and the blenders need
  a dust zone (20/21/22) and explosion protection is the group's to assess
  (README section 9) -- the engine's grammar carries it, the case declares
  none because nobody has done that assessment.

  THE FLOWS (one way each; nothing goes back through a room it left):
    * material in from the WAREHOUSE (south) to goods-in and the dispensary;
      the dispensed kits go in closed containers along the clean corridor
      and through a sink into the GRANULATION room, the coating powders to
      the coating-preparation room;
    * the PRODUCT goes granulation -> drying -> milling -> final blending ->
      compression, out of the suite through a sink into the clean corridor,
      then coating -> packaging -> secondary packaging -> back SOUTH to the
      warehouse;
    * PEOPLE come in at the south lobby (street clothes), gown, and reach
      the work rooms from the clean corridor -- the contained suite through
      a bubble airlock;
    * WASTE (press rejects, dust bags) leaves the contained suite by its
      north return corridor and a sink airlock to the waste room, never
      through a room the product crosses.
  The clean corridor carries people and closed containers of material both:
  declared `shared` below, with the reason.

  EVERY POSITION, SIZE, GRADE AND PRESSURE HERE IS AN AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION
  for the group to replace with its own design; the ISPE Baseline Guide
  Volume 2 (Oral Solid Dosage Forms) is where a real OSD layout is argued,
  cited by title only.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
